  "title": "Laws of Form and discrete physics (Kauffman)",
  "role": "model",
  "summary": "Louis Kauffman: from the bare act of distinction in Spencer-Brown's Laws of Form come imaginary logical values, and from an oscillation between plus and minus one the square root of minus one, iterants, Clifford algebras, fermions and the Dirac equation. Calculus, Hamilton's equations, gauge curvature and a diffusion constant follow from commutators in a noncommutative algebra, with no continuum.",
